 Object ( Bit Out) AND:E( Bit In1, Bit In2) ;  //_GUI 145,36
 Object ( Bit Out) OR:B( Bit In1, Bit In2) ;  //_GUI 152,63
 Object ( Bit Out) INVERT:C( Bit In1) ;  //_GUI 139,62
 Object ( Bit Out1, Bit Out2, Bit Out3) Junction:L
    ( Bit In3) ;  //_GUI 32,64
 Object Text;  //_GUI 143,56,Not Done (Not ADoneIn or Waiting)
 Object Text;  //_GUI 15,145,Clk /
 Object Text;  //_GUI 15,201,Clk /
 Object Text;  //_GUI 11,233,Notes:1. This is a simple case where we are ADoneIn (Asynchronous DoneIn) on the cycle after we are G (Go).  Note that we are Going for one cycle. B (Busy) goes high until ADoneIn comes in high.  Going rises on the clock of G (actually of GoOne) and falls on the clock of ADoneIn.  Once ADoneIn goes high, D (Done) goes high then falls with the rising clock.  GoOne is asynchronous and rises with G then falls with the rising clock.2. This is the same as 1 except that the W (Wait) is on during the G clock.  The W extends the B and delays the D only slightly.  As long as it dropps before the clock, the effective behavior is the same as #1.  3. Same as 2 except W stays on for one more clock.  B is stretched an additional clock and D is delayed one clock.  Note that the ADoneIn still ends the Going, but doesn't produce a D until after the W dropps.4. W comes in high on the cycle that D was going to go out. D goes high with ADoneIn but then goes low when W goes high and stays low until until W goes low again.  B goes high with the clock of G, then goes low with ADoneIn, then goes high with W, then dropps with W.5. In this case DoneIn comes in two clock cycles after G.  We are Going for two cycles.  B stays high until ADoneIn goes high.  D goes high with ADoneIn and drops on the next clock.6. W comes in before G and delays D one clock.7. W comes in after G and delays D one clock.8. Consecutive Gs produce consecutive Ds.9. Attempting consecutive Gs but a W comes in and causes a B and delays the D.  Here the G input disobeys the GDBW protocol and stays on while W is raised, but is ignored.10. Same as 9 except the G input obeys the GDBW protocol by being dropped when W is raised.
 Object Text;  //_GUI 15,173,Clk /
 Object Text;  //_GUI 117,137,The GDBW protocol.Associated data coming out with a Done or coming in with a Go is only valid when the Done/Go is latched high.  It may be changed at any other time unless otherwise specified.Go/Done is ignored when Busy/Wait is high.Go/Done should drop when BusyWait is high.Basically the Go and the Busy are for receiving data and the Done and Wait are for sending data.  If I have data to send, I make the data available on the data lines and raise my Go.  If a Wait comes in during this same clock, I must drop my Go.  Since the receiver's Busy was high, he would not accept the Go (if I hadn't dropped it) and therefore will not take the data.  The data has not yet been received and I must keep it until he drops his Busy (and my Wait).  Once my Wait drops, I raise my Go, the rising edge of the clock then latches in the Go and the Data for the receiver and the receiver has received the data.  I can now prepare to send more data, if I have it and if Wait is low.On the receiving side, I can receive data at any time while my Busy output is low.  If I can't receive data, I should make my Busy high.  Any time I want data, I drop my Busy and I will receive it (with a Go), if there is any available.  If there isn't any available, I won't get a Go.When making a GDBW object, it is highly recommended that you use the GDBW_ADone object or GDBW_Clr object to manage your GDBW protocol rather than using discrete logic.  There are lots of "gotcha"s.One advantage of this implementation over a Wait passthrough to Busy is that each stage of a pipeline built with this object can hold one piece of data before an incoming Wait passes through to the Busy.  That way if your pipeline is not full, the entire pipeline need not stall.  One disadvantage is that there is logic in the Busy/Wait chain and therefore requires that you, now and then, insert a RegOnBusy object which breaks the chain by registering the Busy/Wait.
 Object Text;  //_GUI 11,319,1. The GDBW_Clr object has a one cycle delay between when the DoneIn comes and and when the D (Done) normally goes out.  With this object the D goes out on the same cycle as as the ADoneIn.  Use either GDBW_Clr or GDBW_ADone depending on your needs for (A)DoneIn.2. For both objects, the Going goes high when GoOne is latched high and Going goes low when (A)DoneIn is latched high.  The difference is that DoneIn comes in one cycle sooner than ADoneIn.  So for GDBW_Clr the rising and falling conditions can occur simultaneously and and in that case, Going never goes high.  For this object (GDBW_ADone) the rising and falling conditions are always separated by one clock cycle, so Going is always on for at least one clock cycle (See Note #1).
 Object Text;  //_GUI 10,312,Differences between GDBW_ADone and the GBDW_Clr object:Arial,14,0,0
 Object ( Variant State) State( Variant Enter, Variant Leave
    , Bit ClkG, Bit CLR) ;  //_GUI 93,44
 Object ( Variant State) State:A( Variant Enter, Variant Leave
    , Bit ClkG, Bit CLR) ;  //_GUI 92,85
 Object ( Bit Out1) $Cast:A( Variant Data, Bit Type) ;  //_GUI 112,42

 //_ Behavior Topology
 Output.0 = Junction:A.1;  
 Output:A.0 = Junction:F.1;  
 Output:B.0 = Junction:C.1;  
 Output:C.0 = OR:A.0;  
 AND.0 = INVERT:A.0;  
