 Object ( Bit Out) AND:D( Bit In1, Bit In2) ;  //_GUI 71,82
 Object ( Bit Out) OR:A( Bit In1, Bit In2) ;  //_GUI 57,80
 Object ( Bit Out1, Bit Out2, Bit Out3) Junction:J
    ( Bit In1) ;  //_GUI 53,40
 Object Text;  //_GUI 26,112,Clk /-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_     G __/---\_______/---\_______/---\___________/---\_____________     W______________/---\_______/-------\___________/-------\______DoneIn____/---\_______/---\_______/---\___________/---\____________ GoOne___/---\_______/-\_________/-\_____________/--\______________     B_________________/\__________/----\___________/-------\______ Going_____________________________________________________________     D_____/---\________/--\____________/--\_______/\_______/--\___Notes    1          2           3                4Courier New,8,0,0
 Object Text;  //_GUI 26,166,Clk /-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\     G __/-------\_______/-----------\_______/--\___/----\____     W______________________/---\_______________/---\_________DoneIn____/-------\_______/-----------\_______/--\___/----\___ GoOne___/-------\_______/--\___/----\_______/--\___/----\____     B______________________/---\_______________/---\_________ Going________________________________________________________     D_____/-------\_______/\___/------\_______/\___/------\__Notes    8               9                   10Courier New,8,0,0
 Object Text;  //_GUI 22,198,Notes:1. This is a simple case where we are DoneIn on the same cycle we are G (Go).  Note that we are Going for zero cycles.  When G and DoneIn are latched on the rising clock, D (Done) goes high.  GoOne is asynchronous and rises with G.2. This is the same as 1 except that the W (Wait) input holds the D low until W drops.  In this case W drops before the following cycle and G goes out normally.  B (Busy) goes high with the rising clock, but falls with W and turns out not to effect anything.3. Same as 2 except W stays on for one more clock.  B is streched an additional clock and D is delayed one clock.4. W comes in high on the cycle that D was going to go out.  W shuts off D until W goes low again.5. In this case DoneIn comes in on the clock cycle after G.  We are Going for one cycle.  B stays high for one cycle until DoneIn is latched then D is output.6. W comes in before G and delays D one clock.7. W comes in after G and delays D one clock.8. Consecutive Gs produce consecutive Ds.9. Attempting consecutive Gs but a W comes in and causes a B and delays the D.  Here the G input disobeys the GDBW protocol and stays on while W is raised, but is ignored.10. Same as 9 except the G input obeys the GDBW protocol by being droped when W is raised.
 Object Text;  //_GUI 26,138,Clk /-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_/-\_     G __/---\___________/---\_______________/---\_____________     W__________________/-----------\___________/-------\______DoneIn________/---\___________/---\_______________/---\________ GoOne___/-\_____________/-\_________________/-\_______________     B_____/---\___________/--------\__________/--------\______ Going_____/---\___________/---\_______________/---\___________     D_________/---\________________/--\________________/--\___Notes    5              6                    7Courier New,8,0,0
 Object Text;  //_GUI 124,102,The GDBW protocol.Associated data coming out with a Done or coming in with a Go is only valid when the Done/Go is latched high.  It may be changed at any other time unless otherwise specified.Go/Done is ignored when Busy/Wait is high.Go/Done should drop when BusyWait is high.Basically the Go and the Busy are for receiving data and the Done and Wait are for sending data.  If I have data to send I make the data available on the data lines and raise my Go.  If a Wait comes in during this same clock, I must drop my Go.  Since the receiver's Busy was high, he would not accept the Go (if I hadn't dropped it) and therefore not take the data.  I still have the data and must keep it until he drops his Wait.  Once his wait drops, I raise my Go, the rising edge of the clock then latches in the Go and the Data for the receiver and the receiver has received the data.  I can now prepare to send more data, if I have it and if Wait is low.On the receiving side, I can receive data at any time while my Busy output is low.  If I can't receive data, I should make my Busy high.It is highly recommended that you use this object to manage your GDBW protocol when making a GDBW object rather than using discrete logic.  There are lots of "gotcha"s.One advantage of this implementation is that each stage of a pipeline built with this object can hold one piece of data before an incoming Wait passes through to the Busy.  That way if your pipeline is not full, the entire pipeline need not stall.  One disadvantage is that there is logic in the Busy/Wait chain and therefor requires that you now and then insert a RegOnBusy object which breaks the chain by registering the Busy/Wait.

 //_ Behavior Topology
 Output.0 = Junction:A.1;  
 Output:A.0 = Junction:G.1;  
 Output:B.0 = Junction:D.1;  
 Output:C.0 = Junction.1;  
 State.0 = AND:A.0;  //_GUI 82,46, 82,42
 State.1 = Junction:C.2;  
 State.2 = Junction:H.1;  
 State.3 = Junction:I.0;  //_GUI 36,55
 State:A.0 = AND:D.0;  
 State:A.1 = Junction.0;  //_GUI 83,88, 83,76, 145,76
 State:A.2 = Junction:H.2;  //_GUI 24,91
 State:A.3 = Junction:I.1;  
 AND.0 = INVERT:B.0;  
 AND.1 = Junction:E.1;  
 Junction.0 = $Cast.0;  
 AND:A.0 = Junction:J.1;  
 AND:A.1 = INVERT:A.0;  
 AND:B.0 = Input:A.0;  
 AND:B.1 = INVERT.0;  
 INVERT.0 = Junction:G.0;  //_GUI 27,19, 27,27, 134,27
 Junction:A.0 = AND:B.0;  
 Junction:B.0 = Input:C.0;  
 INVERT:A.0 = Junction:C.1;  //_GUI 33,44
 Junction:C.0 = Junction:B.0;  
 INVERT:B.0 = Junction:F.2;  //_GUI 104,83
 OR.0 = AND:C.0;  
 OR.1 = Junction:D.0;  //_GUI 118,35
 Junction:D.0 = State.0;  
 Junction:E.0 = State:A.0;  
 Junction:F.0 = Input:B.0;  
 Junction:G.0 = OR.0;  
 Junction:H.0 = Input.0;  //_GUI 24,11
 AND:C.0 = Junction:E.0;  //_GUI 101,31
 AND:C.1 = Junction:F.0;  //_GUI 104,34
 Junction:I.0 = Input:D.0;  
 $Cast.0 = AND.0;  
 AND:D.0 = OR:A.0;  //_GUI 70,84, 70,83
 AND:D.1 = Junction:B.1;  
 OR:A.0 = Junction:D.2;  //_GUI 57,64, 118,64
 OR:A.1 = Junction:J.2;  //_GUI 54,85
 Junction:J.0 = Junction:A.2;  
}

